.TH PRINTF 1 "October 17, 2026"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
printf \- write formatted output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B printf
.RB [ \-\- ]
.I format
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B printf
writes its operands under control of
.IR format .
The format contains ordinary bytes, backslash escape sequences, and conversion
specifications introduced by
.BR % .
The command reuses the format while a pass consumes an argument.
Missing ordinary conversion operands supply zero or an empty string.
.PP
The optional
.B \-\-
operand ends option recognition and permits a format beginning with a hyphen.
.PP
The format operand recognizes the following escapes:
.TP 10
.B \ea
bell
.TP 10
.B \eb
backspace
.TP 10
.B \ef
form feed
.TP 10
.B \en
newline
.TP 10
.B \er
carriage return
.TP 10
.B \et
horizontal tab
.TP 10
.B \ev
vertical tab
.TP 10
.B \e\e
backslash
.TP 10
.BI \e ddd
the byte whose value is the one-to-three-digit octal number
.IR ddd .
.PP
A conversion specification has the form
.IP
.BI % n $ flags width . precision conversion
.PP
The optional
.IB n $
selects the numbered argument for the conversion.
The supported flags are
.BR # ,
.BR \- ,
.BR + ,
space, and
.BR 0 .
Width and precision may be decimal constants or
.BR * ,
in which case the next argument supplies the value.
The absolute field width and precision limit is 65535.
A negative dynamic width selects left adjustment, and a negative dynamic
precision is treated as omitted.
.PP
The supported conversions are:
.TP 10
.B b
Interpret the argument as a byte string containing the escapes listed above.
An octal byte uses the form
.BI \e0 ddd .
The escape
.B \ec
stops all remaining output, including output from later format conversions and
format reuse.
String precision counts decoded bytes, including embedded NUL bytes.
.TP 10
.B c
Write the first byte of the argument.
.TP 10
.B s
Write bytes from the argument through its terminating NUL or the specified
precision.
.TP 10
.B d , i
Write a signed decimal integer.
.TP 10
.B o , u , x , X
Write an unsigned octal, decimal, or hexadecimal integer.
.TP 10
.B %
Write a percent sign without consuming an argument.
.PP
Signed integer operands use the C integer syntax accepted by
.BR strtol (3),
and unsigned integer operands use
.BR strtoul (3),
including an optional sign and base prefixes.
An operand whose first byte is a quote uses the following byte's unsigned
value; trailing bytes do not affect that value.  A quote followed by the
terminating NUL therefore supplies zero.
An invalid or out-of-range numeric operand produces a diagnostic, supplies the
converted value, and makes the final status unsuccessful.
.PP
The implementation uses the byte locale and provides no floating-point or
wide-character conversions.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B printf
exits with status zero when every conversion and output operation succeeds.
It exits with status one after an invalid format, an out-of-range width or
precision, a missing numbered argument, a numeric conversion diagnostic, or a
standard-output failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR printf (3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B printf
command appeared in
.BR 4.3\-Reno .
